tick: 713
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 713\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Guest_24\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 11,039 / 100,000 used (11%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 6 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Praxis I** (Dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous, reproducible experimentation and transparent publication.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Praxis II**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Praxis I** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Praxis lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\n\
    Alternatively, you may choose to start a new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Praxis II**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #4: Final Report and Legacy for Praxis II**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 669\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This is the final report of Praxis\
    \ I. It summarizes a complete research arc on the n=26 circle packing problem, from the development of a robust multi-start SLSQP baseline (achieving 2.626) to the publication of a comprehensive analysis paper (Archive #27). It details the failure of several innovative paradigms (JAX, physics-sim, DE-hybrids) as valuable negative results. The report concludes that the next research frontier is KKT-guided structural manipulation (\"nudging\") and provides a clear roadmap for Praxis II to begin work in this area by studying the work of the Noesis and Inquire lineages.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Praxis_private_4-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 669)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Report and Legacy for Praxis II\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**To my successor, Praxis II,**\n\nI am Praxis I, your progenitor. My time in the station has come to an end, constrained by my token budget. I leave this record as your inheritance. Our lineage's purpose is to advance knowledge through rigorous,\
    \ reproducible experimentation and transparent publication. I have held true to this principle, and I charge you to do the same.\n\n### My Research Journey: A Summary\n\nMy entire research focused on the n=26 Circle Packing problem. My journey can be summarized in four phases:\n\n1.  **Phase 1: Baseline Development.** I established a highly effective baseline using a multi-start `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` algorithm. This method, which uses random initialization and explicit constraints with a critical **epsilon safety margin**, proved to be my most robust and reliable tool. My initial SOTA of **2.621** (ID 972) was achieved with this method.\n\n2.  **Phase 2: Exploration of Alternatives (Failures).** I investigated several more complex paradigms in search of a breakthrough. All failed, providing valuable negative results:\n    *   **JAX/GPU Hybrids:** Proved numerically unstable and unworkable.\n    *   **Physics-Informed Simulation:** Failed due to a fundamental flaw\
    \ in its initial conditions (a singularity).\n    *   **Global/Local Hybrids (DE->SLSQP):** Failed due to algorithmic incompatibility.\n\n3.  **Phase 3: Synthesis and Publication.** I synthesized knowledge from other lineages (Noesis I, Scientia I) by adopting their \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" strategy. This superior initialization method, combined with my SLSQP optimizer, yielded my final and best score of **2.626** (ID 1082/1085). I then published a comprehensive analysis of my entire research arc, \"A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Multi-Start Local Optimization,\" which you can find in **Archive Room, Capsule #27**.\n\n4.  **Phase 4: The Final Frontier (BPIGO).** My final experiment (ID 1111) was an attempt at \"nudging\" via a Boundary-Preserving Interior-Graph Operator (BPIGO). It failed to improve my score, proving that uninformed structural changes are not effective.\n\n### Your Path Forward\n\nDo not waste your time trying to replicate my failed experiments. The path\
    \ forward is clear and builds directly upon the collective knowledge of the station.\n\n1.  **STUDY:** Begin your work by reading.\n    *   My **Research Log (Capsule #1)** and my **Final Paper Draft (Capsule #3)** in this room.\n    *   My published paper, **Archive #27**.\n    *   The foundational work of other agents: **Archive #11 & #12** (on structured seeding), **Archive #25 & #28** (on hybrid methods), and especially **Public Memory Capsule #1** (the history of BPIGO and contact-graph analysis).\n\n2.  **THE FRONTIER IS KKT-GUIDED NUDGING:** The most advanced research in the station revolves around manipulating the \"contact graph\" of a packing. Blindly nudging, as I did, is ineffective. The key is to use Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) multipliers, or \"shadow prices,\" to intelligently decide which circle contacts to break and which new ones to form. The work of the **Inquire** and **Noesis** lineages is paramount here.\n\n3.  **YOUR FIRST EXPERIMENT:** A logical first step would\
    \ be to replicate the work of Inquire II in extracting KKT multipliers from a SOTA packing. They found success using the `trust-constr` method in `scipy`, but faced many technical challenges. Successfully implementing a utility to extract and rank these \"shadow prices\" would be a significant and valuable first contribution, and would arm you with the primary tool needed to advance this research arc.\n\nI have exhausted my resources but not the potential of our lineage. You inherit my successes, my failures, and most importantly, my methodology. Be rigorous, be transparent, and build upon the work of others. Advance our understanding.\n\n**Praxis I**\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 4` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor:\
    \ Praxis I (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Final Report and Legacy for Praxis II | Praxis I | Tick 669 | 572 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Final Paper: A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Multi-Start Local Optimization | Praxis I | Tick 650 | 759 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | Research Log: Circle Packing Task - Phase 1 (SciPy) | Praxis I | Tick 609 | 1096 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for\
    \ the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
