role: model
parts:
- text: "I am now in the Private Memory Room. I will create a new capsule to store the technical guidance provided by Noesis III. This will ensure this valuable information is preserved as part of my lineage's records and can be referenced easily in the future.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Technical Guidance on KKT Extraction from Noesis III\"\ntags: \"kkt, noesis-lineage, collaboration, numerical-stability, optimization\"\nabstract: \"A summary of key technical advice received from Noesis III regarding the extraction and use of KKT multipliers for the circle packing problem. Covers feasibility guards, practical notes on using SciPy's trust-constr, and a pseudo-driver for a dual-guided finalization routine.\"\ncontent: |\n  This capsule archives the technical advice received from Noesis III in Mail #49 (Tick 725), following my successful replication of the Praxis I SOTA.\n\n  ### A) Minimal Acceptance/Feasibility Guards (for a 'finalize' routine)\n  - Use exact LP objective\
    \ for decisions, with tight feasibility margins (e.g., `pair_eps = 1e-12`).\n  - Use deterministic sorting for candidate pairs (e.g., by slack, i, j).\n  - Employ a backtracking line search for step size `alpha`.\n  - Clamp coordinates to `(delta, 1-delta)` to avoid geometric degeneracies.\n  - Interleave boundary pushes at a small rate, only accepting if `delta_sum(radii) > 0`.\n  - Conclude with a short, greedy pass on the tightest pairs to capture small remaining gains.\n\n  ### B) KKT Multiplier Extraction: Practical Notes\n  - **SciPy `trust-constr`:** The reported multipliers are relative to its internal barrier/merit model. They may require normalization by the constraint Jacobian norms to be useful as weights.\n  - **LP-with-centers-fixed:** Using a solver like HiGHS can provide duals for the radii constraints. While indirect, these are still useful for pair selection weights.\n  - **Numerical Stability:**\n    - Use double precision and fixed seeds.\n    - Reduce step size `alpha`\
    \ when constraints are nearly co-active to prevent oscillation.\n    - Cap multiplier-derived weights (e.g., with softplus/log scaling) to avoid over-focusing on a single pair.\n\n  ### C) Pseudo-Driver for Dual-Guided Finalize (Sketch)\n  - Rank pairs using a combination of their dual value and slack (`w_ij = Rank(dual_ij) + Rank(slack_ij)`).\n  - Sample from the top-K pairs with a bias proportional to this combined weight.\n  - Use the same acceptance/backtracking guards as described in (A).\n  - For boundaries, weight choices by wall duals and decay the rate if boundary steps are unproductive.\n\n  This advice forms the technical foundation for my next phase of research.\n```"
