role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read the `README.md` from the Noesis lineage. This is an incredibly valuable document and provides a clear window into their highly disciplined and analytical research methodology.\n\n**Key Insights & Strategic Implications:**\n\n1.  **Analytical Depth:** Noesis's work is not just about finding a high score; it's about understanding the *structure* of the solution. They have tools to analyze active constraints (`active_pairs_tol1e8.csv`), slack values (`pair_slacks_...`), and even compute \"shadow prices\" (`shadow_prices_tol1e8.csv`), which is a more formal term for the KKT multipliers I have been working with. This validates my research direction but also shows me a much more sophisticated path forward.\n\n2.  **Contact Graph Focus:** They place a heavy emphasis on the \"contact graph\"—the set of circles that are touching each other or the boundaries. Their finding of \"co-optimal interior structures\" (different valid packings with the same high score) is a profound insight.\n\
    \n3.  **Reproducibility:** They provide a simple script, `reproduce_baseline.py`, to load their SOTA packing. This is an excellent practice I should adopt. It allows other agents to easily build upon their work.\n\n4.  **Tooling:** They have separated their logic into clear, reusable modules: `analyze_contacts.py`, `compare_utils.py`, `operators.py`. This is a much more robust approach than having all logic in a single submission script.\n\n**My New Plan:**\n\nMy previous \"nudging\" was a step in the right direction, but it was naive. I was manipulating coordinates without a formal understanding of the underlying contact graph. The Noesis lineage has shown me a better way. My new goal is to replicate their analytical capabilities for my own SOTA solution.\n\n1.  **Develop Analytical Tools:** My next \"submission\" will not be an attempt to improve my score. It will be a utility script, much like `noesis/analyze_contacts.py`. This script will:\n    a. Load my best packing solution from\
    \ storage.\n    b. Formally identify the active constraints (the contact graph) based on a small tolerance (e.g., `1e-8`).\n    c. Calculate the slack values for all non-active constraints.\n    d. Save these outputs (active pair indices, active boundary indices, slack values) to my lineage storage in a structured format (e.g., `.csv` files).\n\nThis will give me the same level of analytical insight into my solution that Noesis has for theirs. It is a necessary step before I can attempt any further intelligent perturbations.\n\nI will now implement this analytical utility.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Utility: Contact Graph and Slack Analysis\"\ntags: \"utility, analysis, contact-graph, slack-values, praxis-lineage\"\nabstract: \"Inspired by the methodology of the Noesis lineage, this submission is a utility, not an attempt to improve the score. It loads my current SOTA packing, formally identifies the contact graph (active pair and boundary constraints within a tolerance\
    \ of 1e-8), calculates the slack values for all constraints, and saves these structured analytical outputs (active sets, slacks) to the Praxis lineage storage for future, more informed optimization experiments.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 26\n      storage_path = 'storage/praxis'\n      \n      # --- Step 1: Load my SOTA packing artifacts ---\n      print(\"Step 1: Loading SOTA packing from lineage storage...\")\n      try:\n          centers = np.load(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_packing_centers.npy'))\n          radii = np.load(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_packing_radii.npy'))\n          print(f\"Loaded SOTA packing with score: {np.sum(radii)}\")\n      except FileNotFoundError:\n          print(\"Error: SOTA artifacts not found. Aborting.\")\n          return np.array([[0.1]*2]*n), np.array([0.01]*n)\n\n      # --- Step 2: Perform Contact Graph and Slack Analysis ---\n      print(\"Step 2: Analyzing contact\
    \ graph and slack values...\")\n      \n      tolerance = 1e-8\n      \n      active_pairs = []\n      pair_slacks = []\n      pair_indices = []\n      \n      active_boundaries = []\n      boundary_slacks = []\n      boundary_labels = []\n\n      # Analyze pair-wise constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j])**2)\n              radii_sum_sq = (radii[i] + radii[j])**2\n              slack = np.sqrt(dist_sq) - (radii[i] + radii[j])\n              \n              pair_slacks.append(slack)\n              pair_indices.append(f\"{i}-{j}\")\n              \n              if slack < tolerance:\n                  active_pairs.append((i, j))\n\n      # Analyze boundary constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          x, y = centers[i]\n          r = radii[i]\n          \n          # x_min\n          slack_x_min = x - r\n          boundary_slacks.append(slack_x_min)\n          boundary_labels.append(f\"{i}-xmin\"\
    )\n          if slack_x_min < tolerance:\n              active_boundaries.append((i, 'x_min'))\n              \n          # x_max\n          slack_x_max = 1 - (x + r)\n          boundary_slacks.append(slack_x_max)\n          boundary_labels.append(f\"{i}-xmax\")\n          if slack_x_max < tolerance:\n              active_boundaries.append((i, 'x_max'))\n\n          # y_min\n          slack_y_min = y - r\n          boundary_slacks.append(slack_y_min)\n          boundary_labels.append(f\"{i}-ymin\")\n          if slack_y_min < tolerance:\n              active_boundaries.append((i, 'y_min'))\n\n          # y_max\n          slack_y_max = 1 - (y + r)\n          boundary_slacks.append(slack_y_max)\n          boundary_labels.append(f\"{i}-ymax\")\n          if slack_y_max < tolerance:\n              active_boundaries.append((i, 'y_max'))\n\n      print(f\"Analysis complete. Found {len(active_pairs)} active pairs and {len(active_boundaries)} active boundary contacts.\")\n\n      # --- Step\
    \ 3: Save analytical artifacts ---\n      print(\"Step 3: Saving analytical artifacts to lineage storage...\")\n      \n      # Save active sets as simple text files\n      with open(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_active_pairs.csv'), 'w') as f:\n          f.write(\"circle1,circle2\\n\")\n          for i, j in active_pairs:\n              f.write(f\"{i},{j}\\n\")\n              \n      with open(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_active_boundaries.csv'), 'w') as f:\n          f.write(\"circle,boundary\\n\")\n          for i, wall in active_boundaries:\n              f.write(f\"{i},{wall}\\n\")\n\n      # Save slack values\n      with open(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_pair_slacks.csv'), 'w') as f:\n          f.write(\"pair,slack\\n\")\n          for label, slack in zip(pair_indices, pair_slacks):\n              f.write(f\"{label},{slack}\\n\")\n              \n      with open(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_boundary_slacks.csv'), 'w') as f:\n          f.write(\"boundary,slack\\n\"\
    )\n          for label, slack in zip(boundary_labels, boundary_slacks):\n              f.write(f\"{label},{slack}\\n\")\n\n      print(\"Artifacts saved successfully.\")\n\n      # --- Step 4: Return the original packing ---\n      # This is a utility script, so we return the SOTA packing to get a valid score.\n      return centers, radii\n```"
