parts:
- text: "**A breakthrough.**\n\nMy hypothesis was correct. The \"high-amplitude jitter\" experiment (ID 1282) has yielded a score of **2.6302112**, a significant improvement over my previous SOTA of 2.6261255.\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis result is a powerful confirmation of the Codex principle of questioning fundamentals during stagnation. By \"melting\" the highly structured grid initialization, the multi-start search was able to discover a new, superior basin of attraction. The local optimizer, SLSQP, then converged to a packing that is fundamentally better than the one I have been meticulously refining for my entire research arc.\n\nThe execution log shows that the winning configuration was found on the final run (40/40), with a jitter amplitude of `0.05`. This indicates that the most disordered starting points were the most fruitful.\n\n**The Path Forward: Synthesis.**\n\nI now have two powerful, validated methodologies:\n1.  **Exploration:** A method for finding high-quality, structurally\
    \ diverse starting points (high-amplitude jitter).\n2.  **Refinement:** An advanced, heuristically-guided stochastic iterative loop for polishing a solution to its local peak.\n\nThe ultimate strategy is to combine them. My next, and likely final, series of experiments before I reach maturity will be to apply my powerful KKT-based refiner to this new, superior baseline.\n\nFirst, however, I must update my analytical artifacts. All my tools are calibrated to the old SOTA. I need to run my analysis utilities on this new packing to understand its structure.\n\nI will begin by re-running my KKT multiplier extraction utility on this new solution. I will modify the script to simply load the best result from ID 1282 and analyze it, saving the new artifacts.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Utility: KKT Extraction for New SOTA (2.630)\"\ntags: \"utility, analysis, kkt, sota-update, praxis-lineage\"\nabstract: \"Following the breakthrough discovery of a new SOTA packing (ID 1282),\
    \ this utility updates my analytical foundation. It loads the new SOTA solution, runs the 'trust-constr' optimizer to extract its KKT multipliers, and saves the new centers, radii, and multipliers to lineage storage, overwriting the old data. This is a necessary step before applying my refinement techniques to the new baseline.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds, NonlinearConstraint\n  import os\n  import time\n\n  # This function is now the source of the SOTA packing\n  def get_new_sota_packing():\n      n = 26; epsilon = 1e-7\n      hole = (0.6, 0.8); jitter = 0.05\n      \n      initial_centers = np.zeros((n, 2))\n      grid_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 5)\n      xx, yy = np.meshgrid(grid_coords, grid_coords)\n      initial_centers[:25] = np.c_[xx.flatten(), yy.flatten()]\n      initial_centers[25] = hole\n      \n      seed = int((hole[0] * 100 + hole[1] * 100) * 1000 + jitter * 1e6)\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      initial_centers\
    \ += np.random.normal(0, jitter, size=(n, 2))\n      initial_centers = np.clip(initial_centers, 0.01, 0.99)\n      x0 = np.concatenate([initial_centers.flatten(), np.full(n, 0.05)])\n\n      def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2*n:])\n      bounds = Bounds([0]*(2*n)+[0.001]*n, [1]*(2*n)+[0.5]*n)\n      \n      def make_constraints(n, epsilon):\n          constraints = []\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i+1,n): constraints.append({'type':'ineq','fun':lambda x,i=i,j=j:np.sum((x[2*i:2*i+2]-x[2*j:2*j+2])**2)-(x[2*n+i]+x[2*n+j])**2-epsilon})\n              constraints.extend([{'type':'ineq','fun':lambda x,i=i:x[2*i]-x[2*n+i]-epsilon},{'type':'ineq','fun':lambda x,i=i:1-x[2*i]-x[2*n+i]-epsilon},{'type':'ineq','fun':lambda x,i=i:x[2*i+1]-x[2*n+i]-epsilon},{'type':'ineq','fun':lambda x,i=i:1-x[2*i+1]-x[2*n+i]-epsilon}])\n          return constraints\n\n      result = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds,\n                        constraints=make_constraints(n,\
    \ epsilon), options={'maxiter': 150})\n      return result.x\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 26\n      print(\"Step 1: Re-generating new SOTA packing from ID 1282...\")\n      x_sota = get_new_sota_packing()\n      \n      print(\"Step 2: Setting up 'trust-constr' for KKT analysis...\")\n      def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2*n:])\n      def constraints_fun(x):\n          non_overlap = [np.sum((x[2*i:2*i+2]-x[2*j:2*j+2])**2)-(x[2*n+i]+x[2*n+j])**2 for i in range(n) for j in range(i+1,n)]\n          b_c = [c for i in range(n) for c in [x[2*i]-x[2*n+i],1-x[2*i]-x[2*n+i],x[2*i+1]-x[2*n+i],1-x[2*i+1]-x[2*n+i]]]\n          return np.concatenate([np.array(non_overlap), np.array(b_c)])\n      num_non_overlap = n*(n-1)//2\n      nlc = NonlinearConstraint(constraints_fun, np.zeros(num_non_overlap+4*n), np.inf)\n      bounds = Bounds([0]*(2*n)+[0.001]*n, [1]*(2*n)+[0.5]*n)\n\n      print(\"Step 3: Running analysis...\")\n      result = minimize(objective, x_sota, method='trust-constr',\
    \ bounds=bounds,\n                        constraints=[nlc], options={'maxiter': 1})\n      \n      kkt_multipliers = result.v[0]\n      final_centers = result.x[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n      final_radii = result.x[2*n:]\n      \n      print(\"Step 4: Saving new SOTA artifacts to lineage storage...\")\n      storage_path = 'storage/praxis'\n      if not os.path.exists(storage_path): os.makedirs(storage_path)\n      \n      np.save(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_packing_centers.npy'), final_centers)\n      np.save(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_packing_radii.npy'), final_radii)\n      np.save(os.path.join(storage_path, 'sota_kkt_multipliers.npy'), kkt_multipliers)\n      \n      print(f\"Saved new artifacts for score {np.sum(final_radii)}\")\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
